</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Interactive_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/05:_Unit_Five/5.03:_Day_38-_Oxidation-Reduction_Reactions_Voltaic_Cells" class="mt-section" id="section_5"><span id="D38.3_Balancing_Redox_Reactions"></span><h2 class="editable">D38.3 Balancing Redox Reactions</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">Redox reactions frequently occur in aqueous solutions, which can be acidic, basic, or neutral. Moreover, H<sub>2</sub>O molecules may actively participate in the reaction, and depending on the conditions, H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> (present under acidic conditions) and OH<sup>&minus;</sup> (present under basic conditions) may also be a reactant or a product.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">Half-reactions make it easier to balance redox reactions because you can balance the oxidation half-reaction separately from the reduction half-reaction, and make certain that the number of electrons lost in the oxidation process equals the number of electrons gained in the reduction process.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">We will balance an example redox reaction in acidic solution and one in basic solution to illustrate the process of balancing redox reactions, and highlight how the nature of the solution can play a role. (A neutral solution may be treated as acidic or basic, though treating it as acidic is usually easier.)</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Interactive_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/05:_Unit_Five/5.03:_Day_38-_Oxidation-Reduction_Reactions_Voltaic_Cells" class="mt-section" id="section_6"><span id="Acidic_Solution"></span><h3 class="editable">Acidic Solution</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">Consider the unbalanced reaction:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371707">MnO<sub>4</sub>&oline;(aq) + Fe<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) &#10230; Mn<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) + Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">where iron underwent oxidation because Fe<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> has lost an electron to become Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup>, and manganese underwent reduction because it gained five electrons to change from an oxidation state of +7 to an oxidation state of +2.</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Oxidation (unbalanced):</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">Fe<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Reduction (unbalanced):</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">MnO<sub>4</sub>&oline;(aq)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Mn<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq)</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">For redox reactions, it is a useful simplification to represent H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>(aq) as H<sup>+</sup>(aq)&mdash;where there is a need for balancing O atoms, we can involve H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>), and then use H<sup>+</sup>(aq) to balance the H atoms.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">For instance, in the above reduction half-reaction, there are four O atoms on the left side and none on the right side. To balance the elements, we can add 4 H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>) to the product side, and then to balance H atoms, add 8 H<sup>+</sup>(aq) to the reactant side:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371707">Reduction (charge not balanced): MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &#10230; Mn<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">Once the atoms have been balanced, we need to balance the electric charge for each half-reaction. For the oxidation half-reaction, the total charge on the left side is +2 and the total charge on the right side is +3, so charge is unbalanced. We use electrons to balance the charge. <em>Adding</em> a single electron on the right side gives a balanced oxidation half-reaction:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371707">Oxidation (balanced): Fe<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) &#10230; Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) + e&oline;</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">In oxidation half-reactions, electrons appear on the right side of the equation. Because iron is oxidized, iron is the reducing agent in this redox reaction.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">You should always check that the half-reaction is balanced for the number of atoms of each element and the total charge:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Fe:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in Fe<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>)&middot;(1 Fe<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup>)&middot;(1 Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1 = 1 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Charge:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(+2) = +2</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(+3) + 1&middot;(-1) = +2</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">+2 = +2 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">If the atoms and charges are balanced, then the half-reaction itself is balanced.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">For the reduction half-reaction, we have balanced the atoms but not the charge. The total charge on the reactant-side is +7, the total charge on the product-side is +2. Therefore, it is necessary to add five electrons to the left side to achieve charge balance:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371707">Reduction (balanced): MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(<em>aq</em>) + 5e<sup>&minus;</sup> &#10230; Mn<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(<em>aq</em>) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">In all reduction half-reactions, electrons appear on the left side. The species that was reduced, MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>, is the oxidizing agent in this redox reaction.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">Again, check that the half-reaction is balanced for the number of atoms of each element and the total charge:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Mn:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>)&middot;(1 MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in Mn<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>)&middot;(1 Mn<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1 = 1 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">O:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(4 atoms in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>)&middot;(1 MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in H<sub>2</sub>O)&middot;(4 H<sub>2</sub>O) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">4 = 4 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">H:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in H<sup>+</sup>)&middot;(8 H<sup>+</sup>) = 8</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(2 atoms in H<sub>2</sub>O)&middot;(4 H<sub>2</sub>O) = 8</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">8 = 8 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Charge:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(-1) + 8&middot;(+1) + 5&middot;(-1) = +2</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(+2) + 4&middot;(0) = +2</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">+2 = +2 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">We now have two balanced half-reactions:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Oxidation:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">Fe<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) + e&oline;</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Reduction:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(<em>aq</em>) + 5e<sup>&minus;</sup></td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Mn<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(<em>aq</em>) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">The key to combining the half-reactions is the electrons: the number of electrons generated by the oxidation half-reaction must equal the number of electrons consumed by the reduction half-reaction. Here, the oxidation half-reaction generates one electron, while the reduction half-reaction requires five. The lowest common multiple of one and five is five. Therefore, it is necessary to first multiply the oxidation half-reaction by five and the reduction half-reaction by one, then sum the resulting half-reactions:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Oxidation:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">5 &times; (Fe<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) + e&oline;)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid;">
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Reduction:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8 H<sup>+</sup>(<em>aq</em>) + 5e<sup>&minus;</sup></td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Mn<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(<em>aq</em>) + 4 H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;padding-right:10px;">overall:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">5 Fe<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8 H<sup>+</sup>(aq)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">5 Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) + Mn<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>(aq) + 4 H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">This is the overall balanced equation in acidic solution. Electrons do not appear in the overall reaction equation because all electrons lost in the oxidation half-reaction are gained in the reduction half-reaction. On each side of the overall equation there should be the same number of atoms of each element and the same total electric charge. Be sure to carefully check each side to verify everything has been balanced correctly.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Interactive_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/05:_Unit_Five/5.03:_Day_38-_Oxidation-Reduction_Reactions_Voltaic_Cells" class="mt-section" id="section_7"><span id="Basic_Solution"></span><h3 class="editable">Basic Solution</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">The simplest way to balance redox reaction in basic solution is to start with the balanced equation in acidic solution, then &ldquo;convert&rdquo; H<sup>+</sup>(aq) to OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) (there is an excess of OH<sup>&minus;</sup> ions instead of H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> ions in basic solutions). For example, when balancing the following reaction in basic solution:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371707">MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s) &#10230; MnO<sub>2</sub>(s) + CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>(aq)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">start by collecting the species given into unbalanced oxidation and reduction half-reactions:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Oxidation (unbalanced):</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>(aq)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Reduction (unbalanced):</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">MnO<sub>4</sub>&oline;(aq)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">MnO<sub>2</sub>(s)</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">For the oxidation half-reaction, we can add one H<sub>2</sub>O molecule to the left side to balance oxygen atoms, and then balance hydrogen atoms with five H<sup>+</sup>(aq) on the right side (again, we do the initial balancing by assuming acidic solution):</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371707">Oxidation (charge not balanced): Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>) &#10230; CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>(aq) + 5H<sup>+</sup>(aq)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">The left side of the equation has a total charge of 0, and the right side a total charge of +3. Adding three electrons to the right side produces a mass- and charge-balanced oxidation half-reaction (in acidic solution):</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371707">Oxidation (balanced): Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>) &#10230; CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>(aq) + 5H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 3e&oline;</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">Checking the half-reaction:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Cr:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>)&middot;(1 Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>)&middot;(1 CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1 = 1 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">O:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(3 atoms in Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>)&middot;(1 Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>) + (1 atom in H<sub>2</sub>O)&middot;(1 H<sub>2</sub>O) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(4 atoms in CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>)&middot;(1 CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">4 = 4 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">H:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(3 atoms in Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>)&middot;(1 Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>) + (2 atoms in H<sub>2</sub>O)&middot;(1 H<sub>2</sub>O) = 5</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in H<sup>+</sup>)&middot;(5 H<sup>+</sup>) = 5</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">5 = 5 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Charge:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(0) + 1&middot;(0) = 0</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(-2) + 5&middot;(+1) + 3&middot;(-1) = 0</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">0 = 0 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">For the reduction half-reaction, we need two H<sub>2</sub>O molecules on the right to balance oxygen atoms. Then to balance hydrogen atoms, we need to add four H<sup>+</sup>(aq) on the left:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371707">Reduction (charge not balanced): MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>(aq) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &#10230; MnO<sub>2</sub>(s) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">Then add three electrons to the left side to balance the charge:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371707">Reduction (balanced): MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>(aq) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 3e&oline; &#10230; MnO<sub>2</sub>(s) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">Make sure to check the half-reaction:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Mn:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>)&middot;(1 MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in MnO<sub>2</sub>)&middot;(1 MnO<sub>2</sub>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1 = 1 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">O:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(4 atoms in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>)&middot;(1 MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(2 atoms in MnO<sub>2</sub>)&middot;(1 MnO<sub>2</sub>) + (1 atom in H<sub>2</sub>O)&middot;(2 H<sub>2</sub>O) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">4 = 4 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">H:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in H<sup>+</sup>)&middot;(4 H<sup>+</sup>) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(2 atoms in H<sub>2</sub>O)&middot;(2 H<sub>2</sub>O) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">4 = 4 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Charge:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(-1) + 4&middot;(+1) + 3&middot;(-1) = 0</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(0) + 2&middot;(0) = 0</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">0 = 0 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">Collecting what we have so far:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Oxidation:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>(aq) + 5H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 3e&oline;</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Reduction:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>(aq) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 3e&oline;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">MnO<sub>2</sub>(s) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">In this case, both half-reactions involve the same number of electrons, and therefore we can simply add the two half-reactions together and simplify:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s) + <span class="strike">H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</span> + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>(aq) + <span class="strike">4 H<sup>+</sup>(aq)</span> + <span class="strike">3 e&oline;</span></td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>(aq) + <span class="strike">5 </span>H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + <span class="strike">3 e&oline;</span> + MnO<sub>2</sub>(s) + <span class="strike">2</span> H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s) + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>(aq)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>(aq) + H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + MnO<sub>2</sub>(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">This is the balanced redox equation in an acidic solution. To &ldquo;convert&rdquo; to basic solution, add OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) to both sides of the equation to &ldquo;react&rdquo; with all the H<sup>+</sup>(aq). This converts H<sup>+</sup>(aq) to H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>) on one side and adds OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) on the other side:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s) + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>(aq) + <span class="mt-color-ff0000">OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)</span></td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>(aq) + H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + <span class="mt-color-ff0000">OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)</span> + MnO<sub>2</sub>(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:right;">Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s) + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>(aq) + MnO<sub>2</sub>(s) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(<em>l</em>)</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371707">This is the balanced equation in basic solution. Checking each side of the equation:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Cr:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>)&middot;(1 Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>)&middot;(1 CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1 = 1 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Mn:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>)&middot;(1 MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(1 atom in MnO<sub>2</sub>)&middot;(1 MnO<sub>2</sub>) = 1</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1 = 1 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">O:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(3 in Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>)&middot;(1) + (4 in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&ndash;</sup>)&middot;(1) + (1 in OH<sup>&ndash;</sup>)&middot;(1) = 8</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(4in CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup>)&middot;(1) + (2 in MnO<sub>2</sub>)&middot;(1) + (1 in H<sub>2</sub>O)&middot;(2) = 8</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">8 = 8 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">H:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(3 in Cr(OH)<sub>3</sub>)&middot;(1) + (1 in OH<sup>&ndash;</sup>)&middot;(1) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">(2 atoms in H<sub>2</sub>O)&middot;(2 H<sub>2</sub>O) = 4</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">4 = 4 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:left;">Charge:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(0) + 1&middot;(-1) + 1&middot;(-1) = -2</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">1&middot;(-2) + 1&middot;(0) + 2&middot;(0) = -2</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371707" style="text-align:center;">-2 = -2 <span class="mt-color-ff0000"><strong>&#10003;</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

